To achieve this, you can use a combination of HTML, CSS, and JavaScript. Here's an example:

HTML:
```html
<div class="input-container">
  <input type="text" id="search-input" placeholder="Search">
  <svg class="search-icon" viewBox="0 0 24 24" width="24" height="24">
    <path d="M15.5 14h-.79l-.28-.27C15.41 12.59 16 11.11 16 9.5 16 5.91 13.09 3 9.5 3S3 5.91 3 9.5 5.91 16 9.5 16c1.61 0 3.09-.59 4.23-1.57l.27.28v.79l5 4.99L20.49 19l-4.99-5zm-6 0C7.01 14 5 11.99 5 9.5S7.01 5 9.5 5 14 7.01 14 9.5 11.99 14 9.5 14z" />
  </svg>
</div>
```

CSS:
```css
.input-container {
  position: relative;
  display: flex;
  align-items: center;
}

.input-container input {
  padding-right: 30px; /* Adjust this value to control the spacing between the input and the icon */
}

.search-icon {
  position: absolute;
  right: 10px;
  cursor: pointer;
  fill: #999; /* Set the color of the icon */
}
```

JavaScript:
```javascript
const searchInput = document.getElementById('search-input');
const searchIcon = document.querySelector('.search-icon');

searchIcon.addEventListener('click', () => {
  // Call your function here
  handleSearch(searchInput.value);
});

function handleSearch(query) {
  // Implement your search logic here
  console.log(`Searching for: ${query}`);
}
```

In this example, the HTML structure includes an input field and an SVG icon (a reading glass icon). The CSS positions the icon on the right side of the input field and makes it clickable.

The JavaScript code adds a click event listener to the search icon, which calls the `handleSearch` function with the current value of the input field.

You can customize the CSS styles to adjust the appearance of the input field and the icon, as well as modify the JavaScript code to implement your desired search functionality.

